Fix: pathFound (bool) is now pathProblem (string). It returns "not found", "is a directory", "is not executable", or "" when the path starts. warnIfMissing logs "WARN <label>: path <why>: <path>". "not found" gives the old line byte for byte, so the existing exact-message test TestWarnIfMissingAddsNoExtensionElsewhere still passes. Outside Windows: a failed os.Stat is "not found", as before (an unreadable parent directory counts too). Then IsDir is "is a directory", then exec.LookPath(path) is "is not executable". With a "/" in the path, LookPath checks only that file and does not search PATH. The Windows branch is unchanged: any exec.LookPath error is "not found", so the line is the same. Severity stays WARN.

The judgment call, my choice, written in the comment: "executable" means what exec.LookPath decides. That is access(2) X_OK for the process's effective user, falling back to "any x bit" only where access is unavailable (ENOSYS, or EPERM under seccomp). A file that only another user may execute therefore warns, and execve refuses it too. I used this rule instead of "any x bit" because it is still one stdlib call and needs no build tag or uid arithmetic. It also matches what execve does, a noexec mount included.

Test: TestWarnIfMissingNamesWhyAPathDoesNotStart, skipped on Windows. It covers an executable file (no warning), a 0644 file ("path is not executable: <path>", exact) and a 0755 directory ("path is a directory: <path>", exact; its x bits show the directory check is separate). Each case first runs exec.Command(path).Run() and asserts it fails exactly when a warning is expected, so the test checks its premise against exec itself. It was red before the fix: both causes logged nothing.

Mutation sweep with go test -overlay on helpers.go, worktree untouched: 7 mutants, 6 killed. Killed: the fix reverted (the pre-change file), no directory case, no execute check, LookPath on every OS, causes swapped, the old fixed "path not found" message. Survived, as expected: "any x bit" (fi.Mode()&0o111) instead of LookPath. Only a file owned by another user, or a uid change, tells the two apart, which a deterministic test cannot set up without root. The chosen rule is documented, not pinned.

